1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Before calculating motion, choose an origin and a positive direction. Every position, displacement and velocity is then described relative to that reference frame.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Suppose a learner walks twenty metres east, then five metres west. The distance is twenty-five metres. The displacement is positive fifteen metres, or fifteen metres east. Path length and change in position are not the same quantity.

3
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:50,000
A negative velocity means motion in the chosen negative direction. It does not automatically mean slowing down. Acceleration measures how quickly velocity changes, so signs must stay consistent from the sketch to the final answer.

4
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:10,000
On this position-time graph, A to B has a positive gradient, so velocity is positive. B to C is horizontal, so the object is at rest. C to D has a negative gradient, so the object moves back toward the origin.

5
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:29,000
On a velocity-time graph, gradient gives acceleration. The signed area between the graph and time axis gives displacement. Here the object accelerates, moves at constant velocity, and then slows uniformly to rest.

6
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:49,000
You can test these ideas with a toy car, a straight track, a metre rule and a stopwatch. Record the time at fixed positions, average repeated runs, plot position against time, and use a suitable gradient to estimate velocity.

7
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Now complete the guide examples, practical activity and worksheet before attempting the mini-assessment. Check every answer for a formula, substitution, unit, direction and a physically sensible result.
